WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.233
A robot can propose several plausible actions, but choosing
among them requires anticipating their consequences.

2
00:00:07.233 --> 00:00:11.053
Seemingly plausible actions can lead to different task outcomes.

3
00:00:11.053 --> 00:00:14.553
To address this ambiguity, we propose PatchVLA.

4
00:00:14.553 --> 00:00:22.367
It forecasts each candidate's manipulation process, connecting
action generation, sequence evaluation, and latent search.

5
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:29.517
Each forecast tracks interaction phase, local subtask
progress, and the probability of crossing a subtask boundary.

6
00:00:29.517 --> 00:00:36.130
Aligned with the candidate's actions, these signals describe
how contact, manipulation, and transitions may unfold

7
00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.300
throughout the proposed sequence.

8
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.525
A process-conditioned actor generates action chunks,

9
00:00:41.525 --> 00:00:45.460
and a sequence critic evaluates each chunk with its aligned forecast.

10
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:51.660
An MPPI-like update uses candidate costs
to refine the latent proposal distribution.

11
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:58.667
The controller selects one complete evaluated
candidate, executes a prefix, then observes and replans.

12
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:02.557
To learn this process-aware controller, we first prepare training data.

13
00:01:02.557 --> 00:01:08.807
Experts provide a small set of anchors, then a vision-language
model extends the annotations for human review.

14
00:01:08.807 --> 00:01:12.167
Demonstrations train the actor and process forecasts,

15
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:16.433
while recorded candidate outcomes train and calibrate the sequence critic.

16
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:23.013
We evaluate a shared problem: choosing actions that advance
a multi-stage task as interactions and subgoals change.

17
00:01:23.013 --> 00:01:28.608
Our two benchmarks examine this problem in parallel
domains: deformable garments and rigid objects.

18
00:01:28.608 --> 00:01:34.993
LeHome-Fold uses LeHome Challenge folding tasks,
with sleeves and torso as successive milestones.

19
00:01:34.993 --> 00:01:39.213
Candidate forecasts describe the process along each proposed action sequence,

20
00:01:39.213 --> 00:01:42.167
while latent refinement guides the next proposal.

21
00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:50.207
In the complementary rigid-object setting, LIBERO-Long uses the
LIBERO-10 tasks to study the same multi-stage decision problem.

22
00:01:50.207 --> 00:01:53.897
Here, the robot places a soup can, then retrieves tomato sauce.

23
00:01:53.897 --> 00:01:59.487
Across observations, local progress rises, resets
after the first placement, and rises again.

24
00:01:59.487 --> 00:02:03.917
At this decision, C1 is selected for its lower combined planning cost,

25
00:02:03.917 --> 00:02:06.533
despite its slightly lower critic score.

26
00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:11.683
The broader evaluation spans four garment
categories, from sleeves to trouser legs,

27
00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:14.863
with the predicted interaction phase shown during each rollout.

28
00:02:14.863 --> 00:02:22.003
It also covers all ten LIBERO-Long tasks, involving
placement, articulated objects, and sequential goals.

29
00:02:22.003 --> 00:02:28.567
Together, these examples show how the same process
representation follows varied geometries and interactions.

30
00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:33.077
These examples are reflected in the quantitative results.

31
00:02:33.077 --> 00:02:39.852
PatchVLA achieves 76.25% overall success
on LeHome-Fold and 70% on unseen garments,

32
00:02:39.852 --> 00:02:43.533
leading the compared methods on both measures.

33
00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:49.938
On LIBERO-Long, PatchVLA reaches 96.20%
success and 97.70% final new-goal completion.

34
00:02:49.938 --> 00:02:53.858
Beyond success, we measure progress accumulated over time,

35
00:02:53.858 --> 00:02:58.258
retention of achieved goals, and completion without regression.

36
00:02:58.258 --> 00:03:03.093
Command roughness and replanning jumps assess motion continuity,

37
00:03:03.093 --> 00:03:07.100
giving a fuller picture of how the task is completed.

38
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:12.450
To isolate the benefit of search, we compare
refinement with direct candidate selection

39
00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.210
at the same evaluation budget.

40
00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:20.035
Refinement improves LeHome-Fold success by 4.17 percentage points,

41
00:03:20.035 --> 00:03:24.733
showing that how candidates are explored matters alongside how they are scored.

42
00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:29.588
The corresponding comparison on LIBERO-Long gains 2.80 percentage points.

43
00:03:29.588 --> 00:03:34.818
The full method achieves the highest overall success in both benchmarks,

44
00:03:34.818 --> 00:03:39.378
while the component comparisons show that their effects depend on

45
00:03:39.378 --> 00:03:42.133
how prediction, evaluation, and search are combined.

46
00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:49.343
Future work targets better-calibrated forecasts, stronger
critic ranking within each state, and adaptive planning compute,

47
00:03:49.343 --> 00:03:54.700
alongside supervision on executed prefixes and deployment on physical robots.

48
00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:56.250
Thank you for listening.
